00:00:00.679 --> 00:00:05.010
There are several ways in which standardised
working methods benefit the workplace, which

00:00:05.010 --> 00:00:13.870
include reduced waste, increased productivity,
increased customer satisfaction, and compliance.

00:00:14.660 --> 00:00:17.390
In this video, we'll look at
each of these in more detail.

00:00:17.950 --> 00:00:21.520
Let's start with reduced waste.

00:00:22.260 --> 00:00:26.090
There are a few areas within the working
environment where waste can be reduced

00:00:26.100 --> 00:00:27.889
through standardized working methods.

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:33.419
Firstly, standardised working methods can
help ensure that resources are optimised.

00:00:33.970 --> 00:00:34.860
What does this mean?

00:00:35.460 --> 00:00:41.150
By using standardised procedures, the amount of
resources can be measured, thus minimising waste.

00:00:41.900 --> 00:00:45.710
We've previously looked at just in time
ordering for keeping inventory lean.

00:00:46.290 --> 00:00:51.100
Having a standardised process ensures that
only what's needed is ordered, which prevents

00:00:51.110 --> 00:00:53.330
additional inventory and overspending.

00:00:54.610 --> 00:00:57.010
Such processes also help to minimise errors.

00:00:57.860 --> 00:01:02.220
By following set guidelines, employees
are less likely to make errors, thus

00:01:02.220 --> 00:01:06.230
reducing the likelihood of rework and
wasting time, materials, and money.

00:01:07.990 --> 00:01:12.989
Increased productivity, set guidelines,
as we've just seen can be very important.

00:01:13.720 --> 00:01:17.880
It's important that these are clear, well
defined, and employees can follow them.

00:01:18.440 --> 00:01:23.010
When done correctly, guidelines help employees
to carry out their tasks efficiently.

00:01:23.560 --> 00:01:27.570
It minimises thinking time and helps
to ensure tasks are completed to

00:01:27.570 --> 00:01:29.230
the correct standard every time.

00:01:29.780 --> 00:01:35.370
It's easy to see how such systems can reduce
variations and maintain consistency across output.

00:01:37.229 --> 00:01:43.550
Customer Satisfaction Consistency, as we've
just looked at, ensures that products are

00:01:43.550 --> 00:01:46.190
produced to the same standard time after time.

00:01:46.730 --> 00:01:50.999
This builds trust for the customer who know
that they can rely on good quality every time.

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:55.310
Not only is product quality
reliable, but so is time.

00:01:55.870 --> 00:01:59.920
We've already seen that standardised
working methods reduce time, and this

00:01:59.950 --> 00:02:03.740
also ensures that customers will get
their product when they expect it.

00:02:04.290 --> 00:02:09.259
Meeting customer expectations in this
way ensures that trust is built up, and

00:02:09.259 --> 00:02:13.680
the customer is consistently satisfied
with the product and service, resulting

00:02:13.690 --> 00:02:15.940
in repeat business and positive reviews.

00:02:17.810 --> 00:02:18.510
Compliance.

00:02:19.299 --> 00:02:24.839
In Unit 1 we saw how important legislation,
procedures and processes are to businesses.

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:30.099
Often, an industry will need to comply
with certain regulations to ensure that

00:02:30.100 --> 00:02:32.600
they remain safe and offer good quality.

00:02:33.160 --> 00:02:37.169
Think of an electrician, they must be
qualified to carry out electrical work

00:02:37.479 --> 00:02:39.269
and work to set safety guidelines.

00:02:39.909 --> 00:02:43.939
When this is not done, organisations
face heavy fines and they are

00:02:43.939 --> 00:02:45.709
unlikely to get repeat business.

00:02:46.294 --> 00:02:51.695
Standardised processes help an organisation
to identify potential risks and ensure

00:02:51.695 --> 00:02:54.785
that the necessary controls are in
place to manage them effectively.

